  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong on PromiseGrid-style boundary discipline and explicit pCID dispatch, but only an indirect answer to malformed UDP datagrams because the draft defines a DAG-CBOR grid envelope rather than the UDP-feed binding and leaves invalid top-level parse behavior mostly implicit.",
    "strengths": [
      "Payload bytes remain opaque until the pCID handler, which supports the scenario's demand not to invent higher-layer meaning.",
      "The draft explicitly avoids a central pCID registry and treats unknown pCID data as unsupported and unverified rather than magically resolved.",
      "Canonical DAG-CBOR bytes and explicit signature coverage are auditable and straightforward to implement."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "It is not a UDP-feed specification, so it does not directly promise that arbitrary datagrams are passed upward unchanged.",
      "The unknown-pCID policy only helps after envelope decoding succeeds; totally malformed bytes, truncated arrays, or non-DAG-CBOR inputs are not handled in detail.",
      "Peer-local error recording and transport-layer conformance behavior are left unspecified."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Different implementations may diverge on how malformed bytes are treated before successful envelope parsing.",
      "The unknown-best-effort salvage path can blur the line between exposing raw bytes and inferring unsupported semantics."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "If a datagram is not valid DAG-CBOR or not a four-slot envelope, how are raw bytes preserved and audited?",
      "What exact UDP-feed binding guarantees byte preservation into this envelope layer?",
      "Should parse failure, unknown pCID, and bad signature produce distinct audit records?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
